WEBVTT

00:00:00.084 --> 00:00:03.007
Not every concept needs its own table.

00:00:03.007 --> 00:00:07.892
A concept often deserves a separate entity when it has its own identity, several descriptive facts,

00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:11.787
relationships to other concepts, or is reused many times.

00:00:11.787 --> 00:00:16.837
For example, a service has a code, a name, a price, and may appear in many appointments.

00:00:16.837 --> 00:00:20.742
That makes service a strong candidate for its own table.

00:00:20.742 --> 00:00:24.152
A simple attribute usually just describes another entity.

00:00:24.152 --> 00:00:24.532
Birth date describes a patient.

00:00:24.532 --> 00:00:27.912
Appointment start time describes an appointment.

00:00:27.912 --> 00:00:30.092
Quantity describes one service line.

00:00:30.092 --> 00:00:36.852
When deciding, ask whether the concept needs to exist independently and be referenced by other records.

00:00:36.852 --> 00:00:42.177
If not, it may be better represented as a column rather than a separate table.

00:00:42.177 --> 00:00:47.042
Before drawing tables, write one sentence that defines what one row represents.

00:00:47.042 --> 00:00:48.882
That grain statement prevents many later problems.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:55.512
If one row represents a patient, repeated encounters do not belong as extra columns in the same row;

00:00:55.512 --> 00:00:58.320
they are a different entity with a one-to-many relationship.
